								<div class="title" id="post-title-4769095">
																		SCP Foundation? What SCP Foundatin? This is the PCS Foundation!
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/misha-kastrilevich" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6563672); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6563672&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607225800" alt="Misha Kastrilevich" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6563672)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/misha-kastrilevich" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6563672); return false;" >Misha Kastrilevich</a></span> <span class="odate time_1599457641 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">07 Sep 2020 05:47</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4769095">
									

<p>Hello Hello! This is just a general thought that I have in my mind.</p>
<p>We all love our wonderful SCP Foundation universe. We love the way the system work and the anomalous objects. So much so that we can potentially recognize those same things in this other works.</p>
<p>There is this piece of media (I won't say the name but you can guess) that came out in 2019. It's about a world where anomalous objects exist and there is an American organization that is dedicating to capturing these objects and researching them. In this universe, this organization takes place in a building that mysteriously appeared a long time ago and looks super creepy.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Now when this game came out, a lot of people made comparisons to the SCP Foundation. Rightly so, as it did borrow a few things from the SCP universe. For example, there are several objects that are just ordinary but have weird qualities to them (There is a fridge that you have to stare at or else it starts to kill people). There is also an overwhelming sense of bureaucracy with files upon files of information related to any anomalous object of note.</p>
<p>So here comes the question, is it possible to create a universe where anomalous objects exist there is an organization (or several organizations) that seek to capture them and research them while being distinct from the SCP Universe?</p>
<p>Feel free to discuss this in the thread but I would like to throw in my own two cents. I think the best thing you can do when it comes to making sure your universe is distinct from the SCP universe is <strong><span style="text-decoration: underline;">clarity and specifics</span></strong>. So much of the SCP Universe is shrouded in mystery. While there are a lot of concrete specific definitions for there specific things (<a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/security-clearance-levels" target="_blank">Clearance Levels</a>, <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/task-forces" target="_blank">Mobile Task Forces</a>, etc) there is still a lot of mystery. If you read the <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/o5-command-dossier" target="_blank">O5 Command Dossier</a>, you don't see descriptions for one person who is specifically labeled O5-X, you see an entire list of people who could potentially be O5-X. Hell, you can see a note saying that the O5 council might not be real.</p>
<p>When I say that clarity and specifics can be used for distinction, what I mean is that you can have a lot of explanations and worldbuilding in your universe. How did these objects come to be? Why are they like that? Are there multiple reasons why or is there just one big reason why? How did the world react to them? Do people like these objects or not? All of these standard worldbuilding questions can be used to make your world feel more different from the SCP Universe. Specifics and clarity make your idea yours.</p>
<p>It also helps not to straight-up steal concepts from the website. I know that might seem kinda hard since there is so much content on the site (5000+ entries and so on), you might accidentally rip off someone but as someone who has an entire list of SCP ideas on a Google Doc and who also see's which ideas have been done before (Spoiler alert, a lot of them haven't been done yet), I think it is much easier to make an idea and add specifics to that idea in order to make it your idea than to just take something wholesale from the site. Especially if you are just stealing from the Hall of Fame section.</p>
<p>What do you guys think? Feel free to discuss this!</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
